---
<Base page="learn">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="kicker">Primer · about 10 minutes</p>
    <h1>LNG trading basics: netbacks, arbitrage and European gas storage explained</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      This guide is for readers who know finance but are new to gas. It explains how US liquefied natural gas (LNG)
      reaches Europe and Asia, how traders decide where a cargo goes, and why European storage levels move prices.
      Numbers marked <em>illustrative</em> are round figures for teaching, not market data.
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
      <h2 class="toc-title">Contents</h2>
      <ol>{toc.map(([id, t]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{t}</a></li>)}</ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="markets">1. What are Henry Hub, TTF and JKM?</h2>
    <p>
      Natural gas trades at three main benchmark prices. <strong>Henry Hub</strong> (HH) is the US price, set at a
      pipeline hub in Louisiana and quoted in dollars per million British thermal units ($/MMBtu). <strong>TTF</strong>
      is the main European price, set at the Dutch virtual trading point and quoted in euros per megawatt hour (€/MWh).
      <strong>JKM</strong> (Japan Korea Marker) is the North-east Asian spot price for LNG, quoted in $/MMBtu.
    </p>
    <p>
      The three prices can sit far apart because gas is hard to move. Pipelines work well over land but cannot cross
      oceans economically. To ship gas between continents, it is cooled to about −162 °C, where it becomes a liquid
      with roughly 1/600th of its gas volume. That is LNG.
    </p>
    <p>The LNG supply chain has three steps:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Liquefaction</strong>: a plant near the gas field cools the gas and loads it onto a ship.</li>
      <li><strong>Shipping</strong>: a specialised tanker carries it, often for weeks.</li>
      <li><strong>Regasification</strong>: an import terminal warms it back to gas and sends it into the local grid.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Two delivery terms matter. <strong>FOB</strong> (free on board) means the buyer takes the cargo at the loading port
      and pays for shipping. <strong>DES</strong> (delivered ex ship) means the seller delivers to the buyer's terminal and
      bears the shipping. A trader comparing destinations converts DES prices back to an FOB value. That value is the
      netback.
    </p>
    <p>To compare TTF with $/MMBtu prices: $/MMBtu = €/MWh × EUR/USD ÷ 3.412, since one MWh is 3.412 MMBtu.</p>

    <h2 id="us-cargo">2. How does a US LNG cargo make money?</h2>
    <p>
      Most US export plants use a <strong>tolling model</strong>. The buyer pays for the feed gas at a mark-up to Henry
      Hub, typically 115% of HH, which covers the gas plus fuel used in liquefaction. On top, the buyer pays a fixed
      <strong>liquefaction fee</strong> per MMBtu. The fee is owed whether or not the buyer lifts the cargo. Contract fees
      vary; public filings and trade press put recent levels at roughly $2.25 to $3.50/MMBtu.
    </p>
    <p>
      In return the buyer gets <strong>destination flexibility</strong>. Once loaded FOB, the cargo can go wherever it
      earns most: Europe, Asia, Latin America or elsewhere. This flexibility is what links the three gas prices.
    </p>
    <p class="try"><a href={netback}>Try it in the netback calculator</a>: the cost of supply line uses this formula.</p>

    <h2 id="option">3. Why is a US LNG contract like an option?</h2>
    <p>
      A US offtake contract behaves like a series of <strong>call options</strong>. Each month the holder can lift a cargo
      and earn the overseas netback, paying 115% of HH for the gas. The liquefaction fee is the <strong>premium</strong>:
      paid up front, whatever happens. The payoff is the overseas netback minus 115% of HH, if positive.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the lifting decision ignores the fee, because it is already sunk. An <em>illustrative</em> example: HH is
      $3.00, so feed gas costs $3.45. If the best netback is $3.20, lifting loses $0.25/MMBtu on top of the fee. The
      holder cancels the cargo and pays only the fee.
    </p>
    <p>
      This happened at scale in 2020. As COVID-19 cut demand, European and Asian prices fell close to US costs, and
      buyers cancelled many US cargoes. The US Energy Information Administration estimated about 46 cargoes were
      cancelled in June 2020 and about 50 in July
      (<a href="https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=44697">EIA, August 2020</a>).
    </p>

    <h2 id="netback">4. What is an LNG netback?</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>netback</strong> is the destination price minus the cost of getting the cargo there. It answers: what is
      this cargo worth at the US loading port if I send it to market X?
    </p>
    <p class="formula">FOB netback = destination price − shipping cost − boil-off cost</p>
    <p>
      The <strong>arb</strong> (arbitrage) is the difference between the best Asia netback and the Europe netback. When
      it is positive, Asia pays more after shipping. When it is negative, Europe wins.
    </p>
    <p>An <em>illustrative</em> example, all in $/MMBtu:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Illustrative netback example" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Illustrative</th><th scope="col" class="num">Europe</th><th scope="col" class="num">Asia</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Destination price</th><td class="num">12.00</td><td class="num">12.80</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Shipping cost</th><td class="num">0.80</td><td class="num">1.60</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Boil-off cost</th><td class="num">0.15</td><td class="num">0.30</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">FOB netback</th><td class="num"><strong>11.05</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>10.90</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Asia pays $0.80 more at the destination, but the longer voyage costs $0.95 more. Europe wins by $0.15. A higher
      destination price does not always mean a better destination.
    </p>
    <p class="try"><a href={netback}>Try it in the netback calculator</a>: it also shows the JKM level where the arb flips.</p>

    <h2 id="shipping">5. What drives LNG shipping cost?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Charter rate</strong>: the daily hire of the ship, in $/day. It swings widely with fleet supply and
        demand, and is the biggest single driver of freight.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Round trip</strong>: the charterer pays for the voyage out and, usually, the empty (ballast) return. A
        longer route costs twice over.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Route</strong>: from the US Gulf to Japan via the Panama Canal is the shortest. Suez is longer, and the
        Cape of Good Hope longer still. Panama can be limited by drought and booking slots. Suez carries security risk
        in the Red Sea. The Cape avoids both canals but adds days.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Canal fees</strong>: charged per transit, so twice per round trip.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Boil-off</strong>: LNG warms slightly in transit and a small share evaporates each day, often used as
        ship fuel. Modern vessels lose roughly 0.1% of the cargo per day, so a long voyage delivers less gas to sell.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="try"><a href={netback}>Try it in the netback calculator</a>: move the charter rate and watch the arb.</p>

    <h2 id="spread">6. Why does the JKM-TTF spread trade around freight?</h2>
    <p>
      US LNG is the world's main source of flexible supply. Each cargo can go to Europe or Asia, so traders send it to
      whichever pays the higher netback. If JKM rises far enough above TTF to cover the extra shipping to Asia, cargoes
      turn east. Europe then gets fewer cargoes, and TTF rises until the gap closes.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a <strong>location spread</strong>. Like the spread between two delivery points for any commodity, it
      tends to trade within a band set by the cost of moving the product between them. For JKM and TTF that band is
      roughly the difference in shipping cost from the US Gulf. When freight rises, the band widens.
    </p>

    <h2 id="storage">7. How does European gas storage work?</h2>
    <p>
      Europe uses far more gas in winter, mostly for heating. Storage sites, such as depleted fields, salt caverns and
      aquifers, absorb the difference. They <strong>inject</strong> from about April to October and
      <strong>withdraw</strong> from about November to March. EU storage holds roughly a third of annual EU gas use
      (<a href="https://agsi.gie.eu/">GIE AGSI+</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      Since 2022 EU law has set a pre-winter <strong>fill target</strong>, 90% from 2023 onwards. Amendments in 2025 keep the
      90% target but allow it to be met at any point between 1 October and 1 December, with some flexibility in difficult
      market conditions, and extend the rules to the end of 2027
      (<a href="https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2025/07/18/gas-storage-council-greenlights-2-year-extension-of-reserves-filling-rules-to-safeguard-winter-supply/">Council of the EU, July 2025</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>31 March level</strong> matters because it is the starting point for the next refill. A low end-of-winter
      level means more gas must be bought over summer, which tends to lift summer prices and the whole curve. The
      <strong>summer-winter spread</strong>, the price of winter gas minus summer gas, pays storage owners to inject.
      When it is narrow or negative, filling storage loses money without policy support.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storage also gets slower as it empties. Withdrawal relies on reservoir pressure, and pressure falls as gas comes out,
      so the daily rate a site can deliver drops late in winter
      (<a href="https://www.eia.gov/naturalgas/storage/basics/">EIA</a>). This site treats about 20% full as the point where
      EU withdrawal capacity falls sharply. A projection that ends below it does not describe a level storage can actually
      reach. It signals a shortfall that prices would have to close, by cutting demand or drawing in more LNG.
    </p>

    <h2 id="stress">8. How do you stress test gas storage?</h2>
    <p>
      Heating demand follows temperature. Analysts measure it with <strong>heating degree days</strong> (HDD): for each
      day, how far the average temperature falls below a base, here 15.5 °C. A day at 5.5 °C has 10 HDD. A day at 18 °C
      has zero.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>stress test</strong> asks: given today's storage, what happens to the end-of-winter level if the weather
      is colder than normal, LNG arrivals fall, or a supply route fails? It is not a forecast. It maps how sensitive
      the outcome is to each risk.
    </p>
    <p class="try"><a href={storage}>Try it in the EU gas storage forecast</a>: compare the four presets.</p>

    <h2 id="connects">9. How it all connects</h2>
    <p>
      The pieces form one loop. A cold European winter draws storage down faster. Low storage raises the risk of
      shortage, so TTF rises. A higher TTF makes Europe the better netback, so flexible US cargoes that would have gone
      to Asia turn to Europe. Asian buyers must then pay more to keep cargoes, so JKM follows. The JKM-TTF spread moves
      to whatever level redirects enough ships.
    </p>
    <p>
      Freight sits in the middle. When charter rates spike, the spread needed to pull a cargo across a longer route
      widens, and the two markets can drift further apart before the arb works.
    </p>
    <p class="try">
      <a href={netback}>Netback calculator</a> · <a href={storage}>EU gas storage forecast</a>
    </p>

    <h2 id="glossary">10. Glossary</h2>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>MMBtu</dt><dd>Million British thermal units, the US and LNG energy unit for prices ($/MMBtu).</dd>
      <dt>MWh</dt><dd>Megawatt hour, the European energy unit. 1 MWh = 3.412 MMBtu.</dd>
      <dt>HDD</dt><dd>Heating degree days: how far the daily mean temperature falls below a base (here 15.5 °C).</dd>
      <dt>FOB</dt><dd>Free on board: the buyer takes the cargo at the loading port and pays for shipping.</dd>
      <dt>DES</dt><dd>Delivered ex ship: the seller delivers to the buyer's import terminal.</dd>
      <dt>Netback</dt><dd>Destination price minus shipping and boil-off costs: the cargo's value at the loading port.</dd>
      <dt>Arb</dt><dd>Arbitrage: here, the best Asia netback minus the Europe netback.</dd>
      <dt>Boil-off</dt><dd>LNG that evaporates during the voyage, as a share of the cargo per day.</dd>
      <dt>Charter rate</dt><dd>The daily cost of hiring an LNG ship, in $/day.</dd>
      <dt>Regas</dt><dd>Regasification: turning LNG back into gas at an import terminal.</dd>
      <dt>TTF</dt><dd>Title Transfer Facility, the Dutch gas hub and Europe's main price benchmark.</dd>
      <dt>JKM</dt><dd>Japan Korea Marker, the North-east Asian spot LNG price benchmark.</dd>
      <dt>HH</dt><dd>Henry Hub, the US gas price benchmark, at a hub in Louisiana.</dd>
    </dl>

